The important part is the order of the two statements. Printing before the recursive call gives descending output. If printing were placed after the call, the numbers would come out in ascending order instead.

Dry run for `n = 4`:

- Print `4`, call with `3`
- Print `3`, call with `2`
- Print `2`, call with `1`
- Print `1`, call with `0`
- Stop at `0`

This version does not change any input data, and `n <= 0` produces no output.

```java
class Solution {
    public void printNumbers(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        System.out.print(n);
        if (n > 1) {
            System.out.print(" ");
        }
        printNumbers(n - 1);
    }
}
```

The method takes `O(n)` time and uses `O(n)` stack space.

This one keeps the output in a `StringBuilder` while the recursive calls run. The helper adds the current number first, then continues with the smaller number. Printing once at the end avoids many separate console writes.

That extra builder uses memory proportional to the number of digits and spaces, so it is a little less space-friendly than printing directly. It still follows the required recursive order.

```csharp
using System.Text;

public class Solution
{
    public void PrintNumbers(int n)
    {
        StringBuilder output = new StringBuilder();
        BuildOutput(n, output);
        Console.Write(output.ToString());
    }

    private void BuildOutput(int current, StringBuilder output)
    {
        if (current <= 0)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (output.Length > 0)
        {
            output.Append(' ');
        }
        output.Append(current);
        BuildOutput(current - 1, output);
    }
}
```

Dry run for `n = 2` builds `"2 1"`, then the call with `0` stops. The recursion takes `O(n)` time and `O(n)` stack space, while the output string also needs `O(n)` space.

A direct recursive call works here. Print the current number first, then call the method with `n - 1`. The recursion stops when `n` becomes zero or negative.

For example, with `n = 3`, the calls print `3`, then `2`, then `1`.

```java
class Solution {
    public void printNumbers(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        System.out.print(n + " ");
        printNumbers(n - 1);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because there is one call for each number.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion call stack.

Here the helper receives the current number to print. It prints that value and sends the next smaller value into the next call. Keeping the stopping check at the top also handles zero and negative input safely.

For `n = 3`, the output is `3 2 1`. The function returns after the call with `0`, so no extra value is printed.

